1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,296
On October 20,

2
00:00:01,296 --> 00:00:04,432
SpaceX reported its third-quarter results.

3
00:00:04,432 --> 00:00:06,449
Revenue was $14.58 billion,

4
00:00:06,449 --> 00:00:08,540
up 161% from a year earlier.

5
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:13,800
But rocket launches made up just 12% of that revenue.

6
00:00:14,010 --> 00:00:16,084
More than half, $7.56 billion,

7
00:00:16,084 --> 00:00:17,882
came from its AI business,

8
00:00:17,882 --> 00:00:20,440
mostly from renting out AI computers.

9
00:00:20,660 --> 00:00:24,790
A space company now makes more money from data centers on the ground.

10
00:00:25,610 --> 00:00:28,860
The day after the report, the stock jumped 7.8%.

11
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:31,837
But the very next day, it fell back.

12
00:00:31,837 --> 00:00:34,440
It closed at $144.8 on October 22.

13
00:00:35,260 --> 00:00:38,455
To be fair, the whole market had a bad day.

14
00:00:38,455 --> 00:00:40,016
The Nasdaq fell 2.6%,

15
00:00:40,016 --> 00:00:42,097
its biggest drop since June,

16
00:00:42,097 --> 00:00:47,150
and the chip index slid 6.7% as Meta fell 7% on AI spending worries.

17
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:49,839
But SpaceX fell 9.1% in a single day,

18
00:00:49,839 --> 00:00:52,050
more than three times the Nasdaq.

19
00:00:52,870 --> 00:00:54,200
The market was weak,

20
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:57,192
but that alone can't explain a drop this big.

21
00:00:57,192 --> 00:00:59,520
Something else hit SpaceX that day.

22
00:01:00,340 --> 00:01:04,351
Stay to the end and you'll learn what SpaceX really makes money from

23
00:01:04,351 --> 00:01:06,120
now, how solid this profit is,

24
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:09,791
and when the next date that could shake the stock is.

25
00:01:09,791 --> 00:01:12,460
Here's a hint: 1.3 billion SpaceX shares.

26
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:13,622
First,

27
00:01:13,622 --> 00:01:16,640
let's look at what kind of company SpaceX has become.

28
00:01:17,460 --> 00:01:19,850
SpaceX started as a rocket company.

29
00:01:20,070 --> 00:01:22,049
By landing and reusing its rockets,

30
00:01:22,049 --> 00:01:24,820
it cut the cost of getting to space dramatically.

31
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:26,958
It's like flying a plane once and throwing it away,

32
00:01:26,958 --> 00:01:27,860
then finally reusing it.

33
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:30,099
According to Elon Musk,

34
00:01:30,099 --> 00:01:34,110
SpaceX carries about 90% of all the cargo mass the world sends to

35
00:01:34,110 --> 00:01:34,480
orbit.

36
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:37,110
It launched 35 rockets in the third quarter alone.

37
00:01:37,930 --> 00:01:40,673
It used those rockets to launch its own satellites and built Starlink,

38
00:01:40,673 --> 00:01:41,810
a satellite internet service.

39
00:01:42,030 --> 00:01:43,114
It connects ships at sea,

40
00:01:43,114 --> 00:01:45,500
airplanes and remote mountain villages to the internet.

41
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:48,469
Then in February this year,

42
00:01:48,469 --> 00:01:51,970
SpaceX acquired Elon Musk's AI company, xAI.

43
00:01:52,180 --> 00:01:55,490
The Grok chatbot and the social media platform X came with it.

44
00:01:56,320 --> 00:01:56,949
Last year,

45
00:01:56,949 --> 00:02:00,847
Starlink was the biggest business at $11.4 billion in revenue,

46
00:02:00,847 --> 00:02:02,922
launches brought in $4.1 billion,

47
00:02:02,922 --> 00:02:04,180
and AI $3.2 billion.

48
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:06,995
But AI lost more than it earned.

49
00:02:06,995 --> 00:02:10,300
Its operating loss for the year reached $6.4 billion.

50
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:14,150
Starlink earned the money, and AI spent it.

51
00:02:14,970 --> 00:02:17,937
But in less than a year, the order flipped.

52
00:02:17,937 --> 00:02:19,799
In the third quarter alone,

53
00:02:19,799 --> 00:02:21,800
AI revenue hit $7.56 billion,

54
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:23,180
overtaking Starlink.

55
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:24,196
In three months,

56
00:02:24,196 --> 00:02:27,130
it earned more than twice last year's full-year AI revenue.

57
00:02:27,950 --> 00:02:30,910
So what exactly did it sell to grow this fast?

58
00:02:31,720 --> 00:02:33,397
The answer is surprisingly simple.

59
00:02:33,397 --> 00:02:34,580
It rented out computers.

60
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:37,896
The rocket company's side shop turned out to be a giant computer

61
00:02:37,896 --> 00:02:38,670
rental business.

62
00:02:39,490 --> 00:02:44,060
SpaceX owns a huge AI data center in Memphis called Colossus.

63
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:46,760
It's packed with Nvidia AI chips.

64
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:50,208
Other AI companies rent this data center.

65
00:02:50,208 --> 00:02:52,708
According to its IPO filing, Anthropic,

66
00:02:52,708 --> 00:02:54,759
the maker of the Claude chatbot,

67
00:02:54,759 --> 00:02:56,490
pays $1.25 billion a month,

68
00:02:56,720 --> 00:03:00,060
and Google pays $920 million a month starting this October.

69
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:03,808
Those two alone add up to about $26 billion a year.

70
00:03:03,808 --> 00:03:06,736
That's more than SpaceX's entire revenue last year,

71
00:03:06,736 --> 00:03:07,540
$18.7 billion.

72
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:11,223
The company says that at current rental prices,

73
00:03:11,223 --> 00:03:14,634
new AI computers pay for themselves in less than a year.

74
00:03:14,634 --> 00:03:16,400
Buy the chips, rent them out,

75
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:18,410
and you break even within a year.

76
00:03:18,620 --> 00:03:22,210
Breaking even in a year is a dream number in the rental business.

77
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:24,911
But the weaknesses are clear too.

78
00:03:24,911 --> 00:03:28,540
Either side can end the Anthropic contract with 90 days' notice.

79
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:33,294
And Elon Musk said the short contract with Anthropic was SpaceX's own

80
00:03:33,294 --> 00:03:33,820
request.

81
00:03:34,030 --> 00:03:36,839
Right now chips are scarce, so rent is high.

82
00:03:36,839 --> 00:03:38,690
But once chips are plentiful,

83
00:03:38,690 --> 00:03:40,030
rents could fall too.

84
00:03:40,850 --> 00:03:43,899
A simple calculation from the contract value suggests nearly half of

85
00:03:43,899 --> 00:03:46,590
third-quarter AI revenue may have come from Anthropic alone.

86
00:03:46,810 --> 00:03:49,380
That's a lot of reliance on a single customer.

87
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:53,090
Now let's see how solid this profit really is.

88
00:03:53,910 --> 00:03:56,400
SpaceX lost $540 million in the second quarter,

89
00:03:56,400 --> 00:03:58,890
then posted a $4.4 billion profit in the third.

90
00:03:59,710 --> 00:04:03,628
But operating income from the core business was $2.79 billion,

91
00:04:03,628 --> 00:04:05,840
less than two-thirds of net income.

92
00:04:06,060 --> 00:04:08,480
The rest came from outside the core business.

93
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:11,539
There's more to look at.

94
00:04:11,539 --> 00:04:14,430
Of Starlink's 55% gross margin,

95
00:04:14,430 --> 00:04:20,120
about 3 percentage points came from a one-time tariff refund,

96
00:04:20,330 --> 00:04:20,853
and Cursor,

97
00:04:20,853 --> 00:04:22,802
the AI coding company acquired in August,

98
00:04:22,802 --> 00:04:24,228
added $608 million in revenue.

99
00:04:24,228 --> 00:04:25,940
That part came from the acquisition.

100
00:04:26,170 --> 00:04:28,983
The company itself warned that fourth-quarter margins would return to

101
00:04:28,983 --> 00:04:29,880
second-quarter levels.

102
00:04:30,700 --> 00:04:33,130
Starlink added 1.6 million new subscribers,

103
00:04:33,130 --> 00:04:35,730
short of the 2.29 million the market expected.

104
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:38,783
Revenue grew 69%, but while AI lifted results,

105
00:04:38,783 --> 00:04:43,110
subscriber growth in the original core business was slower than hoped.

106
00:04:43,940 --> 00:04:47,190
There's a number more important than profit: cash.

107
00:04:48,010 --> 00:04:50,170
In the third quarter, adjusted EBITDA,

108
00:04:50,170 --> 00:04:52,955
earnings before interest, taxes and depreciation,

109
00:04:52,955 --> 00:04:55,229
roughly cash earnings, was $8.8 billion.

110
00:04:55,229 --> 00:04:56,650
In the same three months,

111
00:04:56,650 --> 00:04:59,720
it spent $19.6 billion on data centers and satellites.

112
00:04:59,940 --> 00:05:01,673
And for the fourth quarter,

113
00:05:01,673 --> 00:05:04,047
it plans to spend $35 to $40 billion,

114
00:05:04,047 --> 00:05:06,100
nearly double the third quarter.

115
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:09,070
It's literally launching money like a rocket.

116
00:05:09,880 --> 00:05:12,062
When you spend far more than you earn,

117
00:05:12,062 --> 00:05:14,130
you have to borrow to cover the gap.

118
00:05:14,950 --> 00:05:17,980
In June, SpaceX issued its first corporate bonds,

119
00:05:17,980 --> 00:05:19,340
borrowing $25 billion,

120
00:05:19,560 --> 00:05:23,140
and it's in talks with banks for $40 billion to buy Nvidia chips.

121
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:24,570
On this earnings call,

122
00:05:24,570 --> 00:05:28,090
the CFO said it is discussing several pools of borrowed capital.

123
00:05:28,910 --> 00:05:30,597
The bond market is watching.

124
00:05:30,597 --> 00:05:31,380
SpaceX's CDS,

125
00:05:31,380 --> 00:05:33,790
the insurance premium against a default,

126
00:05:33,790 --> 00:05:36,320
hit record highs several times in October.

127
00:05:36,540 --> 00:05:39,310
Even on the day after earnings when the stock jumped,

128
00:05:39,310 --> 00:05:40,460
this premium edged up.

129
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:42,530
It's not just SpaceX.

130
00:05:42,530 --> 00:05:46,460
Companies worldwide have borrowed more than $450 billion this year

131
00:05:46,460 --> 00:05:49,020
for AI, more than double last year's total.

132
00:05:49,840 --> 00:05:52,103
So back to our first question.

133
00:05:52,103 --> 00:05:55,800
Why did SpaceX fall so much more than the market?

134
00:05:56,620 --> 00:06:00,046
Employees and investors who held shares before the IPO are barred

135
00:06:00,046 --> 00:06:02,102
from selling for a while after listing.

136
00:06:02,102 --> 00:06:03,420
This is called a lock-up.

137
00:06:03,640 --> 00:06:04,341
When it ends,

138
00:06:04,341 --> 00:06:07,360
the number of shares that can be sold jumps all at once.

139
00:06:08,180 --> 00:06:11,167
On October 22, just two days after earnings,

140
00:06:11,167 --> 00:06:15,852
up to 1.3 billion SpaceX shares held by employees and early investors

141
00:06:15,852 --> 00:06:17,210
became free to sell.

142
00:06:17,430 --> 00:06:20,070
That's about 10% of all SpaceX shares,

143
00:06:20,070 --> 00:06:23,474
worth about $190 billion at the October 20 close,

144
00:06:23,474 --> 00:06:25,280
or about 250 trillion won.

145
00:06:25,510 --> 00:06:27,195
On a weak day for the whole market,

146
00:06:27,195 --> 00:06:29,410
the supply of sellable shares suddenly jumped.

147
00:06:30,220 --> 00:06:32,141
What happened at earlier unlocks?

148
00:06:32,141 --> 00:06:34,411
On August 6 the stock actually rose 6%,

149
00:06:34,411 --> 00:06:37,380
and on August 20 and September 9 it fell nearly 4%.

150
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:39,107
It didn't fall every time,

151
00:06:39,107 --> 00:06:41,600
but the market clearly watches these dates.

152
00:06:42,420 --> 00:06:43,722
And it's not over.

153
00:06:43,722 --> 00:06:47,700
Another 328 million SpaceX shares unlock on October 24.

154
00:06:47,930 --> 00:06:50,361
Around December 8, the 180th day since the IPO,

155
00:06:50,361 --> 00:06:53,360
most of the remaining employee and investor shares unlock.

156
00:06:53,570 --> 00:06:56,780
Elon Musk's stake is locked until June 2027.

157
00:06:57,600 --> 00:06:58,273
At the IPO,

158
00:06:58,273 --> 00:07:01,273
about 5% of all shares could trade on the market.

159
00:07:01,273 --> 00:07:05,190
Estimates say that will rise to about a third by early December.

160
00:07:05,420 --> 00:07:08,786
A stock whose price was set by a small float could suddenly have far

161
00:07:08,786 --> 00:07:09,430
more sellers.

162
00:07:10,250 --> 00:07:11,608
Now the biggest question.

163
00:07:11,608 --> 00:07:13,400
Is the stock expensive right now?

164
00:07:14,210 --> 00:07:16,206
At the October 22 close,

165
00:07:16,206 --> 00:07:19,865
SpaceX's market value was about $2 trillion,

166
00:07:19,865 --> 00:07:22,194
or about 2,590 trillion won.

167
00:07:22,194 --> 00:07:25,770
That's 7% above the $135 IPO price in June.

168
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:30,913
The company guided first-quarter revenue next year at $33 to $37

169
00:07:30,913 --> 00:07:31,452
billion,

170
00:07:31,452 --> 00:07:34,620
well above the $22 billion the market expected.

171
00:07:34,830 --> 00:07:36,757
Using the midpoint for a full year,

172
00:07:36,757 --> 00:07:39,180
that's about $140 billion in annual revenue.

173
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:41,173
Even on that revenue,

174
00:07:41,173 --> 00:07:43,462
the market value is about 14 times sales.

175
00:07:43,462 --> 00:07:45,640
Not earnings, but about 14 times sales.

176
00:07:46,460 --> 00:07:47,669
Let's work backward.

177
00:07:47,669 --> 00:07:49,906
Even at a generous 25 times earnings,

178
00:07:49,906 --> 00:07:54,017
it would need to earn $78.2 billion a year to justify today's market

179
00:07:54,017 --> 00:07:54,380
value.

180
00:07:54,600 --> 00:07:56,931
That's about the size of Apple's annual revenue,

181
00:07:56,931 --> 00:07:58,290
and without a single iPhone.

182
00:07:58,520 --> 00:08:00,275
Even at 40 times earnings,

183
00:08:00,275 --> 00:08:02,840
it would need $245 billion in revenue.

184
00:08:03,660 --> 00:08:06,594
From the company's guidance of about $140 billion a year,

185
00:08:06,594 --> 00:08:09,270
revenue would still need to grow about 3 times more.

186
00:08:10,080 --> 00:08:10,580
Of course,

187
00:08:10,580 --> 00:08:12,530
Elon Musk talks about a bigger picture.

188
00:08:12,530 --> 00:08:15,680
He said $1 trillion in revenue in 2028 is no longer impossible.

189
00:08:15,910 --> 00:08:18,857
The company's goal is to reach an annual revenue pace of $100 billion

190
00:08:18,857 --> 00:08:19,370
by year-end.

191
00:08:19,590 --> 00:08:22,340
Elon Musk's targets always fly as high as his rockets.

192
00:08:23,160 --> 00:08:25,682
So expert views are wildly split.

193
00:08:25,682 --> 00:08:30,954
Analyst price targets range from $100 at DZ Bank to $500 at JPMorgan,

194
00:08:30,954 --> 00:08:32,100
a fivefold gap.

195
00:08:32,320 --> 00:08:35,320
It's hard to believe these numbers describe the same company.

196
00:08:35,540 --> 00:08:39,660
It shows how differently people calculate this company's future.

197
00:08:40,480 --> 00:08:41,268
For reference,

198
00:08:41,268 --> 00:08:43,350
its ValueNote score is 21 out of 100.

199
00:08:43,350 --> 00:08:46,220
Its earnings over the past year are still negative,

200
00:08:46,220 --> 00:08:48,190
so no fair value can be calculated.

201
00:08:49,010 --> 00:08:51,550
So how do people see this company?

202
00:08:52,370 --> 00:08:56,179
The bulls say AI computing demand is just getting started,

203
00:08:56,179 --> 00:08:58,740
Starship will cut launch costs further,

204
00:08:58,740 --> 00:09:02,220
and Starlink will connect directly to phones in 2028.

205
00:09:02,450 --> 00:09:05,834
The bears worry about short-term leases that can end anytime,

206
00:09:05,834 --> 00:09:07,998
spending more than twice what it earns,

207
00:09:07,998 --> 00:09:09,940
and the shares that keep unlocking.

208
00:09:10,760 --> 00:09:11,744
One more thing.

209
00:09:11,744 --> 00:09:16,140
Elon Musk holds about 42% of the shares but about 82% of the votes.

210
00:09:16,360 --> 00:09:20,228
It's fair to say major decisions are effectively made by Elon Musk

211
00:09:20,228 --> 00:09:20,580
alone.

212
00:09:21,400 --> 00:09:23,490
A merger with Tesla is another wildcard.

213
00:09:23,490 --> 00:09:25,631
Both companies formed special committees,

214
00:09:25,631 --> 00:09:28,400
but Elon Musk himself said no terms have been agreed.

215
00:09:28,610 --> 00:09:29,470
On Tesla's side,

216
00:09:29,470 --> 00:09:31,460
50% of all shares must vote in favor,

217
00:09:31,460 --> 00:09:33,180
so a quick decision is unlikely.

218
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:35,907
On the space side, the big task is Starship.

219
00:09:35,907 --> 00:09:36,730
On its 14th flight,

220
00:09:36,730 --> 00:09:39,373
it reached orbit for the first time and deployed new Starlink

221
00:09:39,373 --> 00:09:39,850
satellites.

222
00:09:40,060 --> 00:09:42,113
On the 15th flight, expected in November,

223
00:09:42,113 --> 00:09:45,419
it will try to catch both the booster and the ship with the launch

224
00:09:45,419 --> 00:09:46,070
tower's arms.

225
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:48,970
Of course, this analysis could be wrong.

226
00:09:49,790 --> 00:09:51,720
If the AI chip shortage lasts a few more years,

227
00:09:51,720 --> 00:09:54,060
short-term leases become a weapon for charging even more.

228
00:09:54,280 --> 00:09:56,122
If Starship becomes fully reusable,

229
00:09:56,122 --> 00:09:58,070
today's cost math changes completely.

230
00:09:58,290 --> 00:09:59,307
And like Amazon,

231
00:09:59,307 --> 00:10:03,631
some companies that put investment ahead of profit did end up hugely

232
00:10:03,631 --> 00:10:04,330
successful.

233
00:10:05,150 --> 00:10:08,258
And here's the question many of you are probably most curious about.

234
00:10:08,258 --> 00:10:11,000
How much pressure will the December unlock put on the stock?

235
00:10:11,820 --> 00:10:15,667
About 800 million SpaceX shares are estimated to unlock around

236
00:10:15,667 --> 00:10:18,149
December 8, the 180th day after the IPO.

237
00:10:18,149 --> 00:10:20,010
That's about 6% of all shares.

238
00:10:20,230 --> 00:10:22,096
At the October 22 close,

239
00:10:22,096 --> 00:10:24,118
that's about $115 billion,

240
00:10:24,118 --> 00:10:26,140
or about 150 trillion won.

241
00:10:26,960 --> 00:10:27,901
Hard to picture?

242
00:10:27,901 --> 00:10:31,370
SpaceX trades close to 100 million shares a day on average.

243
00:10:31,590 --> 00:10:34,977
So 800 million shares in December means about eight days of normal

244
00:10:34,977 --> 00:10:37,030
trading volume becomes sellable at once.

245
00:10:37,850 --> 00:10:40,093
Of course, not everyone sells when shares unlock.

246
00:10:40,093 --> 00:10:42,610
People who don't need the money right now keep holding.

247
00:10:42,840 --> 00:10:43,688
If only 10% sell,

248
00:10:43,688 --> 00:10:45,634
that's less than a single day's volume.

249
00:10:45,634 --> 00:10:47,580
At 30%, it's about two and a half days.

250
00:10:47,800 --> 00:10:48,506
If half sell,

251
00:10:48,506 --> 00:10:51,600
it's like four days of volume hitting the market at once.

252
00:10:52,410 --> 00:10:54,394
There's academic research too.

253
00:10:54,394 --> 00:10:58,890
A study of 1,948 US listed companies found prices fell about 1.5% on

254
00:10:58,890 --> 00:11:02,197
average in the three days around a lock-up expiry,

255
00:11:02,197 --> 00:11:05,040
and trading volume stayed about 40% higher.

256
00:11:05,260 --> 00:11:06,691
Smaller than you thought?

257
00:11:06,691 --> 00:11:08,809
Because the date is known in advance,

258
00:11:08,809 --> 00:11:10,870
the market often prices it in early.

259
00:11:11,690 --> 00:11:12,789
On the pressure side,

260
00:11:12,789 --> 00:11:15,877
a price above the IPO price tempts holders to take profits,

261
00:11:15,877 --> 00:11:19,540
and the same study found venture-backed companies fell more at unlock.

262
00:11:19,760 --> 00:11:20,554
On the other hand,

263
00:11:20,554 --> 00:11:23,509
if the Starship flight and the December revenue pace come in strong

264
00:11:23,509 --> 00:11:23,994
beforehand,

265
00:11:23,994 --> 00:11:25,978
the stock could even rise on the unlock date,

266
00:11:25,978 --> 00:11:26,640
like in August.

267
00:11:26,860 --> 00:11:27,325
In the end,

268
00:11:27,325 --> 00:11:30,239
the mood that day is likely to move the stock more than the number of

269
00:11:30,239 --> 00:11:30,830
shares itself.

270
00:11:31,650 --> 00:11:33,276
So if you're watching SpaceX,

271
00:11:33,276 --> 00:11:34,790
keep an eye on these dates.

272
00:11:35,610 --> 00:11:40,230
After the October 24 unlock come the 15th Starship flight in November,

273
00:11:40,230 --> 00:11:43,002
Tesla's shareholder meeting on December 3,

274
00:11:43,002 --> 00:11:46,500
and the end of the 180-day lock-up around December 8.

275
00:11:46,720 --> 00:11:49,984
Also check whether December revenue reaches an annual pace of $100

276
00:11:49,984 --> 00:11:50,380
billion.

277
00:11:50,610 --> 00:11:51,038
Finally,

278
00:11:51,038 --> 00:11:54,408
the key is how it funds more than $35 billion in fourth-quarter

279
00:11:54,408 --> 00:11:56,440
spending, and how much its debt grows.

280
00:11:57,260 --> 00:12:00,211
To sum up, SpaceX now makes more money from AI,

281
00:12:00,211 --> 00:12:02,910
mostly computer rentals, than from rockets.

282
00:12:03,130 --> 00:12:05,816
The profit is real but includes one-time items,

283
00:12:05,816 --> 00:12:08,960
and debt is rising as it spends far more than it earns.

284
00:12:09,170 --> 00:12:12,553
And the drop the day after earnings looks like a weak day for tech

285
00:12:12,553 --> 00:12:15,680
stocks combined with the unlock of 1.3 billion SpaceX shares.

